{{Politics of Balisca}}

Balisca's long standing international policy has been developed on the basis of the [[Basic Law: Universal Humanism]] (adopted in 1960), which establishes {{W|pacificism}} (not to be confused with {{W|pacifism}}), [[W:Defensivism|defensivism]], support for {{W|self-determination}}, {{W|Internationalism|international cooperation}} and the peaceful settlement of conflicts as the guiding principles of Balisca's relationship with other countries and multilateral organizations. International policy is handled by the [[Secretariat of International Affairs and Cooperation (Balisca)|Secretariat of International Affairs and Cooperation]] (''Secretaria d'Asuntos Internacionales y Cooperación''; SAIC)

According to the Neutrality Acts, the [[Secretariat Council (Balisca)|Secretariat Council]] has ultimate authority over foreign policy, while the Congress is tasked with reviewing and considering all diplomatic nominations and international treaties, as well as legislation relating to Baliscan overseas policy. Balisca's international policy is a by-product of the country's unique position as a {{W|great power}} in [[Abyala]], the continent's largest [[W:Developed country|advanced economy]], and a "[[W:Soft power|soft power]] superpower". Baliscan policy has generally been based on the principles of {{W|internationalism}} and {{W|multilateralism}}, peaceful dispute settlement, and {{W|non-interventionism|non-intervention}} in the affairs of other countries. Historically, Balisca has largely remained neutral in international conflicts, with the exception of the [[Great Adonian War]].

Following the outbreak of the [[Volisanian Civil War]], the [[Volisanya Boba|emergent republics]] gradually seized control of territories from [[Colonial Balisca|colonial Volisanian authority]]. This early period of independence would be challenged in 1808 by the reunited Volisanian Empire and it's [[Restaurasionistas|loyalists]] in the Abyala as part of the ''[[Reconquesta]]''. Hostile relations with neighboring [[Potocsí]] stemmed from mutual animosity regarding disputed borders that were left undelineated by the [[Treaty of Macaio]], which confirmed the [[Continuation War|victory]] of the [[Northern Confederation]] over Volisanian loyalists in [[Viceroyalty of New Volisania|western Abyala]] and the independence of much of the [[Northern Triangle]]. Potocsí would continue to dispute their mutual border, leading to successive border clashes as part of [[Balisca-Potocsí conflict]]. Potocsí and would go on to invade the [[Marañón basin]] in 1976, sparking the [[Camboriú War]]. As part of the [[Kuluba Accords|1982 Kuluba Accords]], Potocsí has renounced this claim in exchange for Balisca's demilitarization of the territory. Meanwhile, Balisca and [[Sanrobonia]] have avoided direct armed conflict, despite several periods of tense relations. This dates back to the waning years of the [[Kaftian slave trade]] to [[Haitan]] in the early 19th century.

Balisca is founding member of several international organizations, most notably the [[Adonian Community]] (AC), the [[Abyalan Mutual Development Community]], the [[COCO|Concepción Organization of Cooperation]] (COCO), the [[Pan-Andalusian Community]], and [[La Volisanofonia]]. In 2018, Balisca contributed over 120 million dollars to the AC's regular budget.

An increasingly prominent aspect of Balisca's internationalist policy is promotion of decentralized (non-state) [[W:Mutual aid|mutual aid]] to {{W|social development}} assistance [[W:Direct action|directly]] to vulnerable communities globally through [[W:self-managed social centers|autonomous social initiatives]]. Internationalist efforts include expanding access to [[W:Cuban medical internationalism|medical assistance]] through autonomous efforts, education, cultural exchange, [[W:Climate resilience|climate resilience]] and adaptation. This has largely manifested as a quiet and non-confrontational diplomacy based on [[W:Global citizens movement|global citizenship]] and [[W:Global justice movement|global justice]].

Total aid in 2018 was estimated to be around $13 billion per year that includes: technical cooperation of around $5.1 billion ($2.2 billion in 2018 provided directly by International Secretariat), and an estimated $950 million for in-kind expertise provided by Baliscan institutions specializing in technical cooperation. Balisca is the X largest contributor to the [[International Food Assistance Program]] ($2.0 billion). This is in addition to humanitarian assistance and contributions to multilateral development agencies.

In the years immediately following the [[Great Adonian War]], Balisca made a major effort to promote {{W|solidarity}} within the [[global periphery]], which represented over 90 nations, in the face of quickening [[W:Decolonization|decolonization]]. Apart from humanitarian interventions in revolutionary conflicts and civil wars, Balisca made world-wide commitments to social-and economic programs in 40 poor countries. This was made possible by the rapidly improving Baliscan economy in the 1950s and through the 1970s. The largest programs involved major construction projects, in which over 40,000 Baliscans provided technical advice, planning, and training of engineers. Educational programs involved 50,500 teachers. In addition thousands of specialists, technicians, and engineers were sent as advisors to agricultural mining and transportation sectors around the globe. Balisca hosted 100,000 international students, chiefly from [[Kaftia]] and [[Lurando-Abyala]], in health programs and technical schools. Balisca's extensive program of social and medical support to the global periphery achieved widespread international attention.

Balisca historically claimed {{convert|1461597|km2|abbr=on}} in [[Baliscan Arktici|Arktici]], where it has had the world's oldest [[Punto Baliscano|continuous presence]] (dating back to 1883), though all such claims fall under the provisions of the 1961 [[Arktici Treaty]], of which Balisca is a founding signatory and permanent consulting member.

== Policy ==
{{Main|International policy of Balisca|Basic Law: Universal Humanism}}

=== Armed neutrality ===
Balisca adopted a policy of [[W:Armed neutrality|armed neutrality]] regarding international relations in 1951, following the destructive Great Adonian War and the founding of the [[Adonian Community]]. The nature of Baliscan neutrality shifted as [[W:Wars of national liberation|wars of national liberation]] proliferated, and the [[Socialist Bloc]] began to drift apart ideologically. This policy, designed to ensure {{W|armed neutrality|external security}}, was violated by [[Potocsí]] during the [[Camboriú War]], and further challenged by the [[Bight of Ayorin Crisis|Ayori agression against Sanyan]] (see [[Kafto-Ethiopian Charter]]). These conflicts led to the re-examining of the confederal policy of neutrality, ultimately leading to the signing of the quadrilateral [[Mutual Arms and Technology Agreement]] in 1977, considered by some to be an abandonment of the policy of armed neutrality.

More recently some define Baliscan neutrality more broadly, as having "a strong [[W:Humanitarianism|humanitarian]] focus, with a commitment to [[W:Self-determination|self-determination]], [[W:Decolonization|decolonization]], and {{W|responsibility to protect|human rights}}".

==== Arms control ====
Balisca has restrictive arms export controls through the [[Arms Export Control Act (2009)|1967 Arms Export Control Act]], which prohibits arms exports with nations currently under {{W|international sanctions}}, and those in {{W|conflict zone|active conflict zones}}. As such, it accounts for less than 1% of worldwide weapons sales, and exports arms to only two countries: [[Asikyira]] and [[Burawa]]. Conversely, Balisca was ranked as one of the world's smallest arms exporters by the Adonian Community in 2025. It maintained a long-standing policy of prohibiting the export of advanced arms and other other top-secret technologies to countries outside of the [[Mutual Arms and Technology Agreement]], for concerns that they could be replicated or copied.

In 2022, a Congressional report noted increasing concerns about {{W|dual-use technology}} and the use as weapons components of products from major Baliscan export industries such as chemicals, telecommunications equipment, computer chips and software. The [[Arms Export Control Act (2022)]] was proposed to the [[Confederal Congress]] to restrict the export of such products.

